  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how different nitrogen-rich or organic precursors can assemble into polymeric frameworks with very different degrees of order, from poorly ordered chains to highly crystalline porous networks. The top row focuses on carbon nitride–type materials: a one-dimensional “melon” chain built from linked triangular units, and two-dimensional layered variants such as K-PHI and PTI/LiX, where ions appear to sit between or within the stacked triangular motifs. Small molecular precursors such as urea, melamine, and related compounds are shown as feedstocks for these structures.\n\nThe middle panel shows a semi-crystalline covalent triazine framework (CTF), drawn as a ring-like network made from aromatic building blocks, again linked to a simple nitrile-containing precursor.\n\nThe bottom row presents the broader idea of polymerization outcomes. On the left is an amorphous polymer with irregular connectivity and disorder; on the right is a crystalline covalent organic framework (COF) with a regular hexagonal lattice. Arrows and labels emphasize the contrast between kinetic, irreversible growth leading to amorphous solids and thermodynamic, reversible bond formation enabling ordered crystalline frameworks. Overall, the image highlights how precursor choice and reaction reversibility govern dimensionality, crystallinity, and framework order.",
